    "## Model Compilation\n",
    "\n",
    "The BiLSTM model was compiled using the **Adam optimizer**, **Binary Cross-Entropy loss function**, and **Accuracy** as the evaluation metric. These choices were made based on the nature of the dataset and the binary classification problem.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Optimizer: Adam\n",
    "\n",
    "The Adam (Adaptive Moment Estimation) optimizer was selected because it combines the advantages of Momentum and RMSProp. It automatically adapts the learning rate for each parameter, resulting in faster convergence and more stable training. Adam is widely used in Natural Language Processing tasks due to its efficiency and robustness.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Learning Rate: 0.001\n",
    "\n",
    "A learning rate of **0.001** was chosen because it is the default and most commonly recommended value for the Adam optimizer. It provides a good balance between learning speed and training stability. Higher learning rates may cause unstable training, while lower values increase the training time without significant performance improvements for this dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Loss Function: Binary Cross-Entropy\n",
    "\n",
    "The problem was formulated as a **binary classification** task, where each question-option pair is labelled as:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **1** → Correct answer\n",
    "- **0** → Incorrect answer\n",
    "\n",
    "Since the output layer consists of a single neuron with a **Sigmoid activation function**, Binary Cross-Entropy is the most appropriate loss function for measuring the prediction error.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Evaluation Metrics: Accuracy and AUC\n",
    "\n",
    "Two evaluation metrics were used during training:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Accuracy** measures the percentage of correctly classified question-option pairs and provides an intuitive indication of the model's performance.\n",
    "- **AUC (Area Under the ROC Curve)** evaluates the model's ability to distinguish between the positive (correct answer) and negative (incorrect answer) classes across different classification thresholds. Unlike accuracy, AUC is less sensitive to the chosen decision threshold and provides a more comprehensive measure of classification performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Using both metrics allows the model to be evaluated from different perspectives, providing a more reliable assessment of its learning behaviour.\n",
    "\n",
